  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Colloidal particles always carry an electric charge. The nature of this charge is the same on all the particles in a given colloidal solution and may be either positive or negative.&nbsp;</p><p>Negatively charged sol&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>CdS</mi></math></p><p>Positively charged&nbsp;sol&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><msub><mi>xH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p><p>Macromolecular colloids are&nbsp;individual particles that are large enough to be considered colloids&nbsp;(diameter around 100 nm). These particles are made up of chemicals with a high molecular weight.</p><p>Macromolecular colloid&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo></math>Starch</p><p>Gel&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo></math>&nbsp;Cheese</p>",
